7] [17592186199234 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "82c5626f-5ffa-5852-9567-16e71d3f88de" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199234 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198349 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199240 #edb/ref 1061 20423 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199240 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fd96ee0e-953b-5e30-a73d-de454e088881" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199240 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198354 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199369 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199240 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199369 #edb/ref 1061 45271 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199369 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d692d6c4-d307-5093-8885-932a55dd02f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199369 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186199499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199370 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199234 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199370 #edb/ref 1061 45272 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199370 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "152c742b-df15-5eb5-a164-c09238c5b2e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199370 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186199500 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199371 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199369 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199371 #edb/ref 1061 45293 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199371 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "62b86b82-79e2-51cc-abf3-68181195c0c8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199371 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198433 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199499 #edb/ref 1064 "Now, let's consider the intersection of the elliptic paraboloid \n$z=x^2+y^2$\nand the plane \n$z = x+y-\\dfrac{1}{4},$\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/1d/1d4b8db0985d437807f6e580bc6c2c16cacca1ea7c8e49ed4653717cdb3c9e27.png)\n\n \n\nNote that the intersection of the paraboloid and plane is given by the solution to the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ z=x+y-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n- To find the projection of the intersection onto the $xy$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $z$ from the system. The resulting projection curve will be an ellipse, as depicted below.\n\n![](images/52/520b57cf872ede31d4397d59a833c565147d82a45be1d6b2634f82e1effdff8c.png)\n\n \n\n- To find the projection of the intersection onto the $xz$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $y$ from the system. The resulting projection curve will be an ellipse, as depicted below.\n\n![](images/05/059acf1ec5dadaa98b2cd4d4b6c79884e4facc96dd49664abbc247cfba86aaeb.png)\n\n \n\n- To find the projection of the intersection onto the $yz$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $x$ from the system. The resulting projection curve will be an ellipse, as depicted below.\n\n![](images/80/80d3042d21d2e7265a40739ae1531ac30b03fea16fa67609a4e1b0eac8559411.png)\n\n \n\nLet's see some examples." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199499 #edb/ref 1065 7833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199499 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1d0d1b4d-a008-573c-b368-9ab2bfc7f41c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199499 #edb/ref 1067 "Intersections of Surfaces With Planes" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199500 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we wish to find the points of intersection of the elliptic paraboloid \n$z=x^2+y^2$\nand the line with the equation\n$x = -y = -z+1.$\nThe line, paraboloid, and their points of intersection are shown below.\n\n![](images/51/5159ac99a1bc9f73c01df3c2cc2e6fb2093a3d68e00731609d7b62a6fa55149d.png)\n\n \n\nThe trick is to express the equation of the line in parametric form, substitute the parametric equations into the equation of the surface, and solve for the parameter.\n\nSo, first, we write the equation of our line in the parametric form:\n\n![](images/01/01357a5c6b72d7c976765aeed04db8afa3e3a80e13095d9be1ea2f2594ae5bf5.svg)\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nOur intersection points must belong to the line and the surface simultaneously. So, substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ (-t+1) & =(t)^{2}+(-t)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying this equation, we get\n\n$$\n2t^2+t - 1=0.\n$$\n\nThe solutions to this equation are\n$t=\\dfrac{1}{2}$ and $t=-1.$ \n\nSubstituting these values back into the parametric equations of the line\n\n- For $t=\\dfrac{1}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y=-(\\frac{1}{2})=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ z=-(\\frac{1}{2})+1=\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(\\dfrac{1}{2},-\\dfrac{1}{2},\\dfrac{1}{2}).$\n\n- For $t=-1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=(-1)=-1 \\\\ y=-(-1)=1 \\\\ z=-(-1)+1=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(-1,1,2).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(\\dfrac{1}{2},-\\dfrac{1}{2},\\dfrac{1}{2})$ and $(-1,1,2).$ \n\nLet's see another example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199500 #edb/ref 1065 7834 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199500 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "90b0a614-08e3-5d8b-bcf4-28aacc99e1ee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199500 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358144 #edb/ref 1080 "$(2,2,-4)$ only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358144 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358144 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2b903bea-51f1-51ea-a693-5a0c97f98bf4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358146 #edb/ref 1080 "$(-1,-1,-10)$  only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358146 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358146 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2c022eed-8303-5bbd-919a-71ab3a6c95a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186359064 #edb/ref 1080 "$(-2,-2,-12)$   only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186359064 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186359064 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "95eebd9a-0f31-5806-8042-2e3bfb8f6155" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186359578 #edb/ref 1080 "$(1,1,-6)$  and $(2,2,-4)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186359578 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186359578 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d0b68df9-17b2-54d1-b813-125cb95bf18e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186359732 #edb/ref 1080 "$(3,3,-2)$  and $(-1,-1,-10)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186359732 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186359732 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e0f3ad4f-cc75-5a06-aee9-ee2d0acfd6e4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360043 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186358144 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360043 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186358146 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360043 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186359064 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360043 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186359578 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360043 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186359732 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360043 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360043 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360043 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186358144 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360043 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "1d1cf090-377c-5859-8dd5-5396d17112eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360496 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360043 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360496 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94784" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360496 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360496 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the point of the intersection of the elliptic cone $2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ and the line $x=y=\\frac{z+8}{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360496 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e5c485dc-7f99-51a9-8d40-81cda196a56c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360496 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write the equation of our line in parametric form:\n\n$$\nx=y=\\frac{z+8}{2}=t\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=t \\\\ y &=t \\\\ z &=2t-8,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSubstituting the above expressions for $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ into the equation of the elliptic cone, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ 2{t}^{2}+2{t}^{2} & =(2t-8)^{2} \\\\ 4{t}^{2} & =4{t}^{2}-32t+64 \\\\ 32t & =64 \\\\ t & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute our solution back into the equations of the line. For $t=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2 \\\\ y &=2 \\\\ z &=2(2)-8=-4.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $(2,2,-4).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186495599 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{2}=1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495599 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495599 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2903bd31-59f2-5edf-ba78-0b700636216f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495763 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{4}=1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495763 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495763 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "72fcd5f6-74c1-548c-b798-b9f86c8b5a52" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495990 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{2}=1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495990 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495990 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d9e331d6-2b84-5961-a705-7c0440233497" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496016 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{4}=1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496016 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496016 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e4145407-31af-5f0e-be8b-a08fc7a69e9c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496023 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{8}=1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496023 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496023 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e87b73a0-162f-5a86-8eb0-04ffa6648475" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496158 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186495599 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496158 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186495763 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496158 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186495990 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496158 #ed